Relayium — License Index
Copyright (c) 2026 Relayium

Relayium is open core: different directories in this repository are
licensed differently, according to the trust boundary each one sits on.
There is no single license that covers the whole repository — consult
the table below and the `LICENSE` file inside the relevant directory.

| Directory            | License                                   | Full text                          |
|-----------------------|-------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
| `server/`             | GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 (AGPL-3.0-only) | [`server/LICENSE`](server/LICENSE) |
| `web/`                | GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 (AGPL-3.0-only) | [`web/LICENSE`](web/LICENSE)       |
| `apps/`               | Apache License 2.0                        | [`apps/LICENSE`](apps/LICENSE)     |
| `docs/` (including `docs/protocol/`) | Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0) | [`docs/LICENSE`](docs/LICENSE) |
| `scripts/`            | GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 (AGPL-3.0-only) | no separate file — covered by this default |

**Default for everything else:** any file in this repository not covered by
a more specific statement above (root-level config, CI workflows under
`.github/`, and anything added later that isn't given its own `LICENSE`)
is **AGPL-3.0-only** by default. This directory table exists to name the
exceptions — `apps/` (Apache-2.0) and `docs/` (CC BY 4.0) — not to leave a
gap; there is no "not separately licensed" no-man's-land in this
repository.

## Why three licenses

- **AGPL-3.0** on `server/`, `web/`, `scripts/`, and everything else the
  default catches: `server/` and `web/` are the signaling server, relay
  node, CLI, and the Svelte web client — the parts that make up a running
  Relayium *service*; `scripts/` is repository tooling of the same
  character. AGPL's network-use clause means that anyone who modifies this
  code and offers it as a hosted service to others must publish their
  modified source. That is what stops a competitor from taking the whole
  stack — including the SEO article content under `web/scripts/pages/` —
  and standing up a rival hosted service without giving anything back. It
  is still OSI-approved open source: self-hosters keep every right they
  had before (run it, modify it, redistribute it), they just can't turn a
  private fork into an unshared competing service. Grafana, MinIO, and
  Mastodon all made the same call for the same reason.
- **Apache-2.0** on `apps/` (RelayiumKit, the macOS app, and future iOS):
  GPL-family licenses' "no further restrictions" requirement is in tension
  with the App Store's terms — its DRM (FairPlay) and device-count limits
  are exactly the kind of extra restriction the AGPL forbids adding, which
  is why VLC was pulled from the App Store in 2011 under GPLv2. Apache-2.0
  carries no such clause, so the Apple clients can ship there without that
  conflict.
- **CC BY 4.0** on `docs/` (including `docs/protocol/`): a wire protocol
  specification is meant to be implemented by as many people as possible —
  a code license would be the wrong tool for it, since CC BY 4.0 only asks
  for attribution and otherwise gets out of the way of third-party
  implementations, including competing ones. The same reasoning extends
  cleanly to the rest of `docs/` (design notes, testing procedures,
  planning records): documentation prose isn't something we want locked
  under an "all rights reserved" default either, and one license for the
  whole directory avoids a `docs/protocol/`-only statement drifting out of
  sync with the directory around it.

## Prior versions remain MIT

Every version of this repository published before this relicensing was
released under the MIT License, and that grant is irrevocable. Anyone who
already has, or forks, a pre-relicensing copy of this code may continue to
use, modify, and redistribute *that copy* under MIT forever — this change
cannot and does not reach back and revoke it. Relicensing only governs
versions published from this point forward. If you need the last MIT
version, it is the last commit before this file changed, in this
repository's git history.

## Contributions

This project accepts contributions under the Developer Certificate of
Origin (DCO) — see [`DCO`](DCO) and [`CONTRIBUTING.md`](CONTRIBUTING.md).
By signing off a commit (`git commit -s`) you certify you have the right
to submit it under the license that covers the directory it lands in.
